> In Julia, if I have an array of trues and falses, how can I turn those into an array of symbols (say, :buy and :hold)?
**assistant**:
> You can easily convert an array of boolean values (true/false) into an array of symbols (e.g., `:buy` for `true` and `:hold` for `false`) in Julia using a simple comprehension or mapping function. Here’s how you can do it:
> 
> ### Using Comprehension
> 
> ```julia
> bool_arr = [true, false, true, true, false]  # Example boolean array
> symbol_arr = [b ? :buy : :hold for b in bool_arr]
> ```
> 
> ### Using Mapping with `map`
> 
> Alternatively, you can use the `map` function for a more functional style:
> 
> ```julia
> bool_arr = [true, false, true, true, false]  # Example boolean array
> symbol_arr = map(b -> b ? :buy : :hold, bool_arr)
> ```
> 
> ### Example Output
> 
> For both methods, given the `bool_arr`, the `symbol_arr` will result in:
> 
> ```julia
> [:buy, :hold, :buy, :buy, :hold]
> ```
> 
> Choose the method that you find more intuitive! Let me know if you need further assistance!
