> What if I have three options. I have a line that says:
> max.(aas, bbs, ccs)
> so that gives me a vector of that combines the highest values of aas, bbs, and ccs. Now I want a vector that says :aa, :bb, or :cc, depending on which value was chosen.
**assistant**:
> You can achieve that by comparing the three vectors element-wise and generating an output vector of symbols based on which vector has the maximum value. You can use a combination of broadcasting and a conditional comprehension or the `map` function to do this.
> 
> Assuming `aas`, `bbs`, and `ccs` are arrays of numbers, you can follow this approach:
> 
> ### Using a Comprehension
> 
> ```julia
> aas = [3, 1, 4]
> bbs = [1, 5, 2]
> ccs = [2, 2, 3]
> 
> # Determine which symbol corresponds to the max values
> result = [a > b && a > c ? :aa : b > a && b > c ? :bb : :cc for (a, b, c) in zip(aas, bbs, ccs)]
> ```
> 
> ### Using `map` with a Function
> 
> Alternatively, if you prefer a functional approach, you can define a function and use `map`:
> 
> ```julia
> function max_symbol(a, b, c)
>     if a > b && a > c
>         return :aa
>     elseif b > a && b > c
>         return :bb
>     else
>         return :cc
>     end
> end
> 
> result = map((a, b, c) -> max_symbol(a, b, c), aas, bbs, ccs)
> ```
> 
> ### Example Output
> 
> For the above example with `aas`, `bbs`, and `ccs` defined, `result` will yield:
> 
> ```julia
> [:aa, :bb, :aa]  # This will depend on the max values from each position
> ```
> 
> In both methods, you get a vector of symbols indicating which of the three options had the maximum value at each index. Let me know if you have further questions!
